## Spec-driven workflow (Speckit-style)

When working on a feature, follow this order and keep artifacts in sync:

1. `specs/<feature>/spec.md` (what/why; user stories; acceptance)
2. `specs/<feature>/plan.md` (how; architecture; risks; decisions)
3. `specs/<feature>/tasks.md` (ordered, actionable checklist)
4. Implementation PRs that mark tasks complete in `tasks.md`

### Operating rules

- Prefer editing the existing feature files rather than creating parallel docs.
- If you must assume something, write it explicitly in `spec.md` (Assumptions) and reflect it in `plan.md`.
- Keep `tasks.md` as the single source of truth for execution order; update checkboxes as work is completed.
- If a change in implementation invalidates the spec/plan, update the docs immediately.

### Minimal task format (required)

Every task line in `tasks.md` must follow:

- [ ] T001 [P] [US1] Short description with file path(s)

Where:
- `T###` is sequential and ordered
- `[P]` only when parallelizable
- `[US#]` for story-scoped tasks
- include the file path(s) being changed/created

### Suggested Cursor chat prompts

- “Update `specs/<feature>/spec.md` from this description: …”
- “Fill in `specs/<feature>/plan.md` for our stack (Next.js + Postgres).”
- “Generate `specs/<feature>/tasks.md` from spec+plan in strict checklist format.”
- “Implement tasks T00X–T00Y; update checkboxes in `tasks.md`; keep tests/build green.”

